1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,080
To some, it was a miracle.

2
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:10,760
Others called it the triumph of illiteracy.

3
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:14,020
Radio.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:17,460
It revolutionized the way people learned about their world.

5
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:23,360
It changed forever the way people would be entertained.

6
00:00:23,361 --> 00:00:27,980
You've got to straighten out that closet one of these days.

7
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:33,180
Now, out of thin air, the invention of radio on Modern Martins.

8
00:00:53,360 --> 00:01:06,740
Yesterday, December 7th, 1941, a date which will live in infamy.

9
00:01:06,741 --> 00:01:17,600
On December 8th, 1941, 60 million Americans tuned in their radios to hear President Franklin Delano Roosevelt ask Congress to declare war against Japan.

10
00:01:17,601 --> 00:01:19,380
The Empire of Japan.

11
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:26,120
On that day in 1941, radio occupied a position at the very center of American life.

12
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:34,160
People relied on it for information, entertainment, very often religion, and as a lifeline to a world around them.

13
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:44,120
No one knew the power of radio better than Roosevelt, who had used the medium so effectively to reassure Americans during the dark days of the Depression.

14
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:48,720
Now once again they listen to the familiar voice of their radio president

15
00:01:48,721 --> 00:01:54,820
in living rooms, offices, automobiles, restaurants, grocery markets or gas stations.

16
00:01:55,180 --> 00:01:57,680
Radio was everywhere. It was taken for granted.

17
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:02,060
Yet just a few short decades before, it didn't exist.

18
00:02:02,061 --> 00:02:15,520
Most people credit Guillermo Marconi with inventing the radio

19
00:02:15,521 --> 00:02:19,840
and he is recognized as the inventor of the wireless telegraph

20
00:02:19,841 --> 00:02:22,660
filing for his patent in 1896.

21
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:27,780
But brilliant scientist though he was, Marconi had many collaborators

22
00:02:27,781 --> 00:02:34,220
and that he was the beneficiary and exponent of centuries of research in the field of magnetic electricity.

23
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,780
In a sense, Marconi may have discovered radio,

24
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:41,940
but previous discoveries by a host of scientists and great thinkers

25
00:02:41,941 --> 00:02:44,640
had given him a good idea where to look.

26
00:02:44,641 --> 00:02:58,641
Man's desire to wage war created the need to communicate quickly over distance

27
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:02,020
and thereby gain advantage over the enemy.

28
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:07,340
Medieval knights reflected sunlight on their shields as signals.

29
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:10,820
Napoleon soldiers rallied to trumpet and drum.

30
00:03:11,620 --> 00:03:14,440
Native American Indians exchanged smoke signals.

31
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:18,240
while American colonists communicated with flags.

32
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:22,340
In the early part of the 19th century,

33
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:25,000
the discovery and development of the electromagnet

34
00:03:25,001 --> 00:03:29,280
made telegraphy, communication over wire, scientifically possible.

35
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:36,160
In 1835, Samuel Moore sent his first message in dots and dashes

36
00:03:36,161 --> 00:03:38,780
over telegraph wire from Washington to Baltimore.

37
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:40,840
What hath God wrought?

38
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:42,920
By the beginning of the Civil War,

39
00:03:42,921 --> 00:03:47,140
the same country that was about to divide itself in conflict north against south

40
00:03:47,141 --> 00:03:52,580
had been stitched together east to west with the completion of a transcontinental telegraph wire.

41
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:57,160
Two years after the close of the war, a transatlantic cable was laid.

42
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:01,800
Not long after the telegraph lines went up,

43
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:05,740
scientists became aware that electrical current was leaking from the wires

44
00:04:05,741 --> 00:04:09,400
and magnetizing metallic objects at a considerable distance.

45
00:04:09,401 --> 00:04:16,400
The reason for this remained a mystery until 1856 when James Clark Maxwell, an English physicist,

46
00:04:16,401 --> 00:04:22,400
offered mathematical proof that electrical impulses travel through space in the form of waves.

47
00:04:22,401 --> 00:04:28,400
These electromagnetic waves, he wrote, were similar in form and equal in speed to the velocity of light.

48
00:04:28,401 --> 00:04:35,400
Well, I think the history of radio starts with a man named Clark Maxwell in the 19th century

49
00:04:35,401 --> 00:04:40,120
who really stands as one of the most important physicists of all time,

50
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:42,600
and really as important as Newton.

51
00:04:42,900 --> 00:04:49,060
Clark Maxwell postulated the existence of electromagnetic waves,

52
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:52,780
which of course are responsible not only for sound,

53
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:55,880
but for all of the light that we have around us.

54
00:04:56,420 --> 00:05:00,560
In 1887, Heinrich Hertz, a young German scientist,

55
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:02,740
attempted to apply Maxwell's theory.

56
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:07,880
He fashioned an apparatus that had two metal balls separated by a narrow gap.

57
00:05:08,660 --> 00:05:13,740
He connected this device to a coil of wire, a battery, a switch, and a Leiden jar,

58
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:15,780
a device for storing electricity.

59
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:20,660
With the flick of a switch, Hertz caused electrons to jump the gap between the balls

60
00:05:20,661 --> 00:05:24,460
by alternating the polarity of the balls from negative to positive.

61
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:30,380
Hertz called this device an oscillator because the spark oscillated or switched back and forth

62
00:05:30,381 --> 00:05:34,312
continuously as long as the alternating current was maintained.

63
00:05:34,313 --> 00:05:36,060
Experimenting further, Hertz

64
00:05:36,061 --> 00:05:39,256
attached a metal ball to each end of the length of wire.

65
00:05:39,257 --> 00:05:41,140
He fashioned the wire into a loop

66
00:05:41,141 --> 00:05:43,390
but left a narrow gap at the ends.

67
00:05:43,391 --> 00:05:46,700
He then placed the wire loop near but not touching

68
00:05:46,701 --> 00:05:47,566
the oscillator.

69
00:05:47,567 --> 00:05:51,720
When he turned the oscillator circuit on, a spark jumped between the gap

70
00:05:51,721 --> 00:05:52,421
and the loop.

71
00:05:52,422 --> 00:05:56,000
How could this happen, Hertz wondered, when the loop and the oscillator

72
00:05:56,001 --> 00:05:57,585
were not even connected.

73
00:05:57,586 --> 00:06:02,140
The answer, Hertz speculated, lay in the existence of electromagnetic

74
00:06:02,141 --> 00:06:07,343
waves. These waves had traveled through the air from the oscillator to the spark.

75
00:06:07,344 --> 00:06:08,044
This

76
00:06:08,045 --> 00:06:10,920
simple experiment was the first radio transmission.

77
00:06:10,921 --> 00:06:13,720
Hertz's oscillator had been the transmitter

78
00:06:13,721 --> 00:06:17,233
and his wire loop had been the receiver.

79
00:06:17,234 --> 00:06:21,360
Five years later, 18-year-old Guillermo Marconi

80
00:06:21,361 --> 00:06:25,880
he read of Hertz's accomplishments and saw instantly the commercial possibilities of

81
00:06:25,881 --> 00:06:29,692
wireless communication, especially in the shipping industry.

82
00:06:29,693 --> 00:06:31,280
He turned the third floor

83
00:06:31,281 --> 00:06:36,540
of his family home into a workshop, read everything he could get his hands on relative to Hertzian

84
00:06:36,541 --> 00:06:41,160
waves and sought to take up where Hertz and others had left off.

85
00:06:41,161 --> 00:06:44,242
His parents had a very large estate in Italy.

86
00:06:44,243 --> 00:06:47,460
His mother was a Jameson from the Irish Whiskey

87
00:06:47,461 --> 00:06:48,260
company.

88
00:06:48,261 --> 00:06:56,260
And when Marconi could not get the Italian government interested in his wireless

89
00:06:56,261 --> 00:07:03,380
telegraphy, his mother took him to London and connected him with the British postal authorities.

90
00:07:03,860 --> 00:07:10,700
And they saw the potential of what wireless telegraphy would do and immediately snapped

91
00:07:10,701 --> 00:07:11,612
up the patent.

92
00:07:11,613 --> 00:07:16,560
Though untrained as a scientist, Marconi was able through trial and error to

93
00:07:16,561 --> 00:07:21,120
increase the efficiency of Hertz's oscillator. Then he added a grounded

94
00:07:21,121 --> 00:07:25,740
antenna which he found strengthened the waves, although he didn't know why. Later

95
00:07:25,741 --> 00:07:29,180
he determined that by raising his antenna he could increase his

96
00:07:29,181 --> 00:07:33,960
transmitting range. By the time Marconi was settled in England he had

97
00:07:33,961 --> 00:07:40,260
transmitted Morse code without wires, an astounding nine miles. In 1898 he

98
00:07:40,261 --> 00:07:44,700
transmitted the results of a yacht race from a tugboat, allowing newspapers to

99
00:07:44,701 --> 00:07:48,520
print stories of the race before the yachts had returned to shore.

100
00:07:50,040 --> 00:07:53,920
Ship owners began to purchase wireless systems and install them on their vessels.

101
00:07:55,400 --> 00:08:02,360
On March 27, 1899, Marconi sent a wireless signal across the English Channel to France.

102
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:09,180
Overshadowing some of Marconi's success, at least in his own mind, was the fact that he

103
00:08:09,181 --> 00:08:12,908
thought in order to be a success, wireless had to be private.

104
00:08:12,909 --> 00:08:14,680
The telegraph and the fledged

105
00:08:14,681 --> 00:08:18,678
telephone were private, connecting only a single caller and receiver.

106
00:08:18,679 --> 00:08:20,300
But wireless could be picked

107
00:08:20,301 --> 00:08:22,415
up by anyone with a receiver.

108
00:08:22,416 --> 00:08:27,520
Privacy, he felt, was the key to success in communications. But before

109
00:08:27,521 --> 00:08:33,057
signals could be isolated, they would have to be distinguished one from the other.

110
00:08:33,058 --> 00:08:33,800
In England,

111
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:39,640
Sir Oliver Lodge had patented a plan for regulating the wavelength of the transmitter and tuning the

112
00:08:39,641 --> 00:08:46,780
receiver to pick up only that wavelength. Marconi bought the patent from him. As the

113
00:08:46,781 --> 00:08:50,784
new century dawned, the air was filled with wireless signals.

114
00:08:50,785 --> 00:08:52,360
But these consisted only

115
00:08:52,361 --> 00:08:53,896
of dots and dashes.

116
00:08:53,897 --> 00:08:59,880
In 1901, the first transatlantic messages were exchanged by wireless. Four

117
00:08:59,881 --> 00:09:05,400
years later, the SS Arapaho used for the first time the international distress call SOS.

118
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:11,080
Soon afterward, the entire U.S. Navy was equipped with Marconi's equipment.

119
00:09:20,180 --> 00:09:27,980
On Christmas Eve in 1906, professional and amateur wireless operators in the area of Brant Rock, Massachusetts,

120
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:31,120
picked up a transmission they would never forget.

121
00:09:31,660 --> 00:09:34,960
Instead of the usual dots and dashes, they heard human voices

122
00:09:34,961 --> 00:09:38,540
and the strains of a violin playing an old Christmas hymn.

123
00:09:38,820 --> 00:09:42,980
The center of this transmission was a scientist named Reginald Aubrey Fessenden.

124
00:09:42,981 --> 00:09:48,680
When Fessenden first played his violin on Christmas Eve,

125
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:54,540
and the signal operators at first heard this music coming out on their earphones,

126
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:58,780
can you imagine? They thought there was some kind of magic that was happening.

127
00:09:58,781 --> 00:10:05,960
What Fessenden did was he imprinted a voice on a radio wave.

128
00:10:06,080 --> 00:10:12,800
And he did this by creating, with the help of General Electric Company and a man named Ernst Alexanderson,

129
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:16,180
something called the Alexanderson Alternator,

130
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:26,340
which was essentially an electric generator that moved so fast that it created not just electricity but electromagnetic waves.

131
00:10:26,341 --> 00:10:32,540
Pheasanton used a carbon microphone to convert his voice into an audio signal.

132
00:10:33,220 --> 00:10:37,060
His transmission technique was called amplitude modulation or AM

133
00:10:37,061 --> 00:10:40,740
because it modulated or controlled the amplitude or height of the wave.

134
00:10:41,780 --> 00:10:45,860
Pheasanton's Christmas Eve transmission introduced a new concept to the world.

135
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:49,160
A transmission intended for anyone who could hear it.

136
00:10:49,161 --> 00:10:50,160
A broadcast.

137
00:10:51,260 --> 00:10:56,280
And since this new concept suggested an image of waves radiating outward in all directions

138
00:10:56,281 --> 00:10:59,800
from a central point, the term radio was born.

139
00:11:02,080 --> 00:11:06,820
The grand collaboration had resulted in history's most stunning achievement

140
00:11:06,821 --> 00:11:08,400
in the field of communications.

141
00:11:09,100 --> 00:11:11,400
But here is where the cooperation ended.

142
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:16,700
From this point, radio would no longer be a curiosity, a tinker as fancy.

143
00:11:17,060 --> 00:11:21,840
Overnight, it would become big business with fortunes to be made or grabbed.

144
00:11:21,841 --> 00:11:27,500
The coming decade would see bitter rivalries emerge in the struggle to control this new medium.

145
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:33,520
It would see the rise of the powerful media corporation and the demise of the individual inventor.

146
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:39,040
There are nearly twice as many radios in the U.S. as there are people.

147
00:11:39,820 --> 00:11:43,200
