206
00:14:16,180 --> 00:14:19,820
We now return to radio on Modern Marvels.

207
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:27,280
Kessington's broadcast had sent radio technology spinning off in a new direction,

208
00:14:27,960 --> 00:14:34,840
and soon inventors and wireless operators alike were experimenting with ways to more effectively broadcast the human voice.

209
00:14:34,841 --> 00:14:40,240
But a better, smaller generator and a more sophisticated receiver were needed.

210
00:14:41,120 --> 00:14:44,320
Early receivers contained mineral rocks called crystals.

211
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:52,200
These changed the alternating current of the incoming wave into direct current, allowing the sound to be heard.

212
00:14:52,900 --> 00:14:56,140
Crystal sets, as they were called, were inexpensive and popular.

213
00:14:56,920 --> 00:15:01,860
But since they operated solely off the lower power generated by the incoming signal,

214
00:15:01,861 --> 00:15:04,900
the operator had to wear headphones to hear anything.

215
00:15:06,240 --> 00:15:09,740
One of Marconi's technical advisors, John Ambrose Fleming,

216
00:15:09,920 --> 00:15:15,480
realized that building bigger generators was not the key to improve radio transmitters and receivers.

217
00:15:16,820 --> 00:15:20,384
Inspired by Edison's light bulb and the work of English scientist J.J.

218
00:15:20,385 --> 00:15:21,860
Thompson involving electrons,

219
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:27,360
Fleming built a glass valve or diode which was the forerunner of the vacuum tube.

220
00:15:28,240 --> 00:15:33,980
This diode used the power of the electron rather than the mechanical device to capture radio waves.

221
00:15:35,080 --> 00:15:40,140
In 1906, inventor Lee DeForest modified Fleming's diode to create a new device

222
00:15:40,141 --> 00:15:44,260
which amplified the audio signal, making it easier to hear.

223
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:47,260
It was the first electronic amplifier.

224
00:15:47,740 --> 00:15:49,600
He called it the Audion tube.

225
00:15:49,601 --> 00:15:57,200
And he made the invention that changed the world, and that was the radio tube as we know it today.

226
00:15:57,360 --> 00:16:02,020
But the curious thing is, Lee DeForest didn't understand what he had invented.

227
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:11,220
In 1911, a 21-year-old electrical engineer named Edwin Howard Armstrong began to study DeForest's Audion tube.

228
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:18,620
He determined that electrons within moved back and forth or oscillated in a steady rhythm.

229
00:16:19,480 --> 00:16:26,640
This current could be fed back into the system to be amplified again, thereby greatly increasing the power of the signal.

230
00:16:27,360 --> 00:16:33,420
This principle became known as feedback or regeneration and remains a basic principle of radio today.

231
00:16:35,240 --> 00:16:42,480
With Armstrong's innovation, large, expensive generators could be replaced by small vacuum tubes.

232
00:16:43,140 --> 00:16:44,960
Radio became more practical.

233
00:16:44,961 --> 00:16:58,961
On the night of April 14, 1912, the luxury steamship Titanic collided with an iceberg in the North Atlantic and began to sink.

234
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:08,960
The operator of the ship's Marconi wireless system tapped out a desperate call for help.

235
00:17:08,961 --> 00:17:15,960
The liner Carpathia picked up the signal and came to the rescue in time to save 711 passengers.

236
00:17:15,961 --> 00:17:20,960
But 1,500 and 13 others had already perished in the icy water.

237
00:17:20,961 --> 00:17:25,960
For the next few days, a shocked world received news of the tragedy via wireless.

238
00:17:25,961 --> 00:17:30,960
As each passenger was accounted for, the world heard about it.

239
00:17:30,961 --> 00:17:36,960
In Nantucket, Massachusetts, a young wireless operator for the Marconi company, David Sarnoff,

240
00:17:36,961 --> 00:17:39,460
remained at his key for 72 hours,

241
00:17:40,060 --> 00:17:44,920
receiving news of the tragedy from the rescue ship Carpathia as she approached New York.

242
00:17:45,900 --> 00:17:49,220
Sarnoff, a Russian immigrant with only an eighth grade education,

243
00:17:49,520 --> 00:17:52,480
had secured a position with the Marconi Company in New York,

244
00:17:53,100 --> 00:17:55,440
becoming the inventor's personal errand boy.

245
00:17:56,240 --> 00:17:59,800
The subsequent post as key operator at one of Marconi's land stations

246
00:17:59,801 --> 00:18:02,080
had put him in the right place at the right time.

247
00:18:03,020 --> 00:18:09,600
The Titanic tragedy had underscored the importance of radio to humankind in time of emergency.

248
00:18:10,700 --> 00:18:14,300
But David Sarnoff recognized radio's potential as a business.

249
00:18:15,160 --> 00:18:21,900
In 1916, now a young executive, Sarnoff wrote a lengthy memo to the president of American Marconi,

250
00:18:22,380 --> 00:18:25,580
wherein he outlined what he perceived to be the medium's future.

251
00:18:26,580 --> 00:18:31,040
I have in mind a plan of development which would make radio a household utility.

252
00:18:31,041 --> 00:18:35,040
The idea is to bring music into the home by wireless.

253
00:18:35,041 --> 00:18:39,040
The receiver can be designed in the form of a simple radio music box

254
00:18:39,041 --> 00:18:43,040
and arranged for several different wavelengths, which should be changeable

255
00:18:43,041 --> 00:18:47,040
with the throwing of a single switch or pressing of a single button.

256
00:18:47,041 --> 00:18:52,040
Also, events of national importance can be simultaneously announced and received.

257
00:18:52,041 --> 00:18:55,040
This premeditated invasion.

258
00:18:55,041 --> 00:18:59,040
Baseball scores can be transmitted in the air.

259
00:18:59,041 --> 00:19:04,860
This proposition would be especially interesting to farmers and others living in outlying districts.

260
00:19:05,460 --> 00:19:07,480
Sincerely yours, David Sarnoff.

261
00:19:09,980 --> 00:19:13,900
As the specter of World War loomed on America's horizon,

262
00:19:14,480 --> 00:19:18,680
the fledgling radio industry was already engaged in a war of its own.

263
00:19:19,780 --> 00:19:26,100
Powerful corporations dueled in court for the patents that would allow them to gain a stranglehold on the new medium.

264
00:19:26,960 --> 00:19:33,600
No more would inventors be permitted to freely adapt or build upon the work of others for the good of all humanity.

265
00:19:34,420 --> 00:19:38,380
Radio was now a product that could be sold, and there was money to be made.

266
00:19:40,740 --> 00:19:44,860
America's entry into World War I actually helped the radio industry.

267
00:19:45,640 --> 00:19:53,820
The government declared a moratorium on patent suits and resolved disputes by pooling the patents and allowing all companies to draw from the pool.

268
00:19:53,821 --> 00:19:59,300
This allowed the industry to concentrate on development and manufacturing instead of their legal squabbles.

269
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:03,840
Soon radios were standardized and mass-produced to meet wartime demand.

270
00:20:05,300 --> 00:20:12,120
While serving with the Army in Europe, Edwin Howard Armstrong continued his research into high-frequency radio waves.

271
00:20:12,620 --> 00:20:16,820
The result of his work was a more efficient radio receiver he called a super-heterodyne.

272
00:20:17,460 --> 00:20:23,720
The super-heterodyne improved radio reception by narrowing the frequency range handled by amplifiers.

273
00:20:23,820 --> 00:20:30,820
This helped eliminate unwanted electrical interference while still boosting the radio signal.

274
00:20:30,821 --> 00:20:37,820
Armstrong's super heterodyne, though complex in circuitry, was simple to operate.

275
00:20:37,821 --> 00:20:42,820
With just one knob for volume and one for tuning, it was the first modern radio receiver.

276
00:20:42,821 --> 00:20:48,820
At the end of the war, the U.S. government understood how important radio had become

277
00:20:48,821 --> 00:20:52,820
and moved to keep the industry from slipping into foreign hands.

278
00:20:52,821 --> 00:20:58,300
The Radio Corporation of America was formed with General Electric as the parent company.

279
00:20:59,600 --> 00:21:03,680
The patents and assets of American Marconi were transferred to the new company.

280
00:21:04,680 --> 00:21:11,600
In return, British Marconi could continue to buy American parts for the equipment it manufactured and sold to the rest of the world.

281
00:21:12,360 --> 00:21:17,400
Most of American Marconi's employees, including David Sarnoff, moved to RCA.

282
00:21:18,260 --> 00:21:24,000
David Sarnoff still harbored his dream of selling radio music boxes for use in private homes.

283
00:21:24,760 --> 00:21:27,960
But another company, Westinghouse, beat him to his goal.

284
00:21:28,640 --> 00:21:33,460
Westinghouse had purchased the exclusive rights to Armstrong's Super Heterodyne receiver,

285
00:21:33,980 --> 00:21:38,660
and now they determined their best entry would be to broadcast programming from radio towers

286
00:21:38,661 --> 00:21:41,120
and then sell the receivers to the public.

287
00:21:42,120 --> 00:21:43,288
Westinghouse chose Dr.

288
00:21:43,289 --> 00:21:47,480
Frank Conrad to build a radio station on the roof of its factory in Pittsburgh.

289
00:21:48,060 --> 00:21:54,700
Station KDKA went on the air November 2, 1920, broadcasting the presidential election returns.

290
00:21:54,701 --> 00:21:59,080
As soon as they are received by us here at KDKA.

291
00:21:59,740 --> 00:22:01,780
The broadcast was a huge success.

292
00:22:02,220 --> 00:22:04,440
Sales of radio receivers skyrocketed.

293
00:22:04,720 --> 00:22:09,940
In short order, KDKA and other stations began broadcasting church services.

294
00:22:11,120 --> 00:22:12,820
And then boxing matches.

295
00:22:14,780 --> 00:22:17,820
Radio took off and never looked back.

296
00:22:21,540 --> 00:22:27,060
Up until now, communications companies had made their money through two-way communications charges

297
00:22:27,061 --> 00:22:29,940
like the telegraph, telephone, and now the wireless,

298
00:22:30,880 --> 00:22:32,660
poured through the sale of radio equipment.

299
00:22:32,661 --> 00:22:41,340
On August 28, 1922, WEAF in New York broadcast an advertisement for an apartment building.

300
00:22:41,960 --> 00:22:44,080
It was the first paid radio commercial.

301
00:22:44,860 --> 00:22:48,140
Suddenly, a new world of enterprise appeared at radio's door.

302
00:22:50,740 --> 00:22:51,460
Advertising.

303
00:22:54,580 --> 00:22:59,660
Radio broadcasting networks were formed when the Federal Trade Commission ruled that RCA,

304
00:22:59,661 --> 00:23:04,240
RCA, AT&T, and other companies had created a monopoly in the new industry.

305
00:23:05,040 --> 00:23:10,780
The national broadcasting company NBC was formed from RCA and Westinghouse in 1926.

306
00:23:11,780 --> 00:23:15,080
And the Columbia Broadcasting Service followed in 1929.

307
00:23:15,900 --> 00:23:21,100
Both networks were permitted to use AT&T's phone lines to connect their transmitters.

308
00:23:21,101 --> 00:23:26,240
The networks allowed people throughout the country to hear broadcast simultaneously.

309
00:23:27,240 --> 00:23:33,380
Overnight, radio began to affect every American life as no other technological influence ever had.

310
00:23:33,900 --> 00:23:39,760
The sale of radio sets grew so fast that the manufacturers were hard-pressed to keep up with demand.

311
00:23:40,940 --> 00:23:46,340
Radio made the world smaller just that fast because it could be transmitted so quickly

312
00:23:46,341 --> 00:23:50,240
and you didn't have to wait for the newspapers to let you know what was happening.

313
00:23:50,241 --> 00:23:57,580
In January 1925, 15 million people heard Calvin Coolidge's inaugural speech on their radios.

314
00:23:57,920 --> 00:24:02,260
So many advertisers flocked to the new medium that some newspaper chains panicked

315
00:24:02,261 --> 00:24:06,640
and refused to carry radio listings or even mentioned the word radio in their pages.

316
00:24:07,760 --> 00:24:12,320
In 1927, the first coast-to-coast program was broadcast on NBC.

317
00:24:12,880 --> 00:24:14,820
It was the Rose Bowl football game.

318
00:24:14,821 --> 00:24:18,101
Probably the biggest radio event of the 20s was Charles A.

319
00:24:18,102 --> 00:24:21,100
Lindbergh's historic solo flight across the Atlantic.

