Julie from Maumee, Ohio.

722
00:52:41,180 --> 00:52:45,640
I think people live vicariously through other people's lives through radio.

723
00:52:46,780 --> 00:52:48,380
And radio broadcasters know this.

724
00:52:48,381 --> 00:52:56,444
They know by putting people on the air, just listeners on the telephone, giving their opinion that they will capture the imagination of people just listening

725
00:52:56,445 --> 00:52:57,145
vicariously.

726
00:52:57,320 --> 00:53:03,060
As more listening alternatives appeared on the radio dial, the audience became more and more fragmented.

727
00:53:05,680 --> 00:53:12,120
More choices for advertisers meant that stations had to compete harder for their share of the advertising dollars.

728
00:53:12,740 --> 00:53:16,940
Rating services like Arbitron and Birch began to poll the listening public

729
00:53:16,941 --> 00:53:19,880
to find out who was listening and to what station.

730
00:53:20,900 --> 00:53:24,540
The broadcast industry broke its audience down into categories

731
00:53:24,541 --> 00:53:26,920
according to age and sex and income.

732
00:53:27,680 --> 00:53:29,820
The analysis was called demographics.

733
00:53:30,860 --> 00:53:33,680
Today, radio is really ruled by this, Arbitron.

734
00:53:34,480 --> 00:53:39,920
Arbitron is the rating service that is used to measure ratings for radio.

735
00:53:39,921 --> 00:53:46,300
The ratings will indicate who's number one, number two, number ten, number fifteen in different age groups.

736
00:53:47,040 --> 00:53:54,000
And the advertising community uses that tool to decide how much advertising or whether to place advertising on radio stations.

737
00:53:54,960 --> 00:54:00,100
There's very few stations left where the disc jockeys pick their own music.

738
00:54:00,960 --> 00:54:05,340
And I'm reluctant to burst that myth, but that's the case.

739
00:54:05,341 --> 00:54:13,140
The music is pre-programmed into a computer, and the computer is told by a music director how to rotate the songs.

740
00:54:14,280 --> 00:54:17,500
What is happening is people want immediate gratification.

741
00:54:18,300 --> 00:54:23,380
They don't want to have to wait for one type of music form to end before they can get another

742
00:54:23,381 --> 00:54:27,960
when they know they can press button number two on the radio and get that exact music form.

743
00:54:27,961 --> 00:54:37,480
I think the rating system was perhaps the most crippling influence on not only radio but television.

744
00:54:38,920 --> 00:54:43,760
I think there has been altogether a dumbing down, and that's a terrible thing to happen.

745
00:54:43,900 --> 00:54:46,820
It's after 8 o'clock. We're here. We're queer. Get used to it.

746
00:54:46,821 --> 00:54:52,720
It's strange, isn't it, that in a lifetime you could see the birth and the demise of an industry as we knew it.

747
00:54:53,040 --> 00:54:57,020
Of course, radio is stronger today than it ever was so far as commercials and whatnot are concerned.

748
00:54:57,021 --> 00:55:03,300
In the future, you're going to have an all-blue station, an all-reggae station, an all-hip-hop station.

749
00:55:04,000 --> 00:55:09,040
And it's reflective of the sophistication of the rating service.

750
00:55:10,600 --> 00:55:19,240
In 1962, the satellite Telstar was launched into orbit, and the communications networks were boosted into space.

751
00:55:19,241 --> 00:55:24,780
Soon dozens of satellites were orbiting the earth

752
00:55:24,781 --> 00:55:28,200
relaying and amplifying signals from one station to another

753
00:55:28,201 --> 00:55:33,300
Phone conversations, radio and television programming

754
00:55:33,301 --> 00:55:37,400
computer data, all signals riding on radio waves

755
00:55:37,401 --> 00:55:41,080
Today life without radio would be unimaginable

756
00:55:41,081 --> 00:55:44,080
We use it for information and entertainment

757
00:55:44,081 --> 00:55:46,320
Radio waves cook our food

758
00:55:46,321 --> 00:55:52,120
open our garage doors and allow us to communicate down the block or far into deep space.

759
00:55:54,940 --> 00:56:00,240
Remarkable that Marconi registered his patent for wireless barely 100 years ago.

760
00:56:01,100 --> 00:56:05,460
Perhaps the reason we take radio for granted is that we understand instinctively

761
00:56:05,461 --> 00:56:08,400
that it has always been here in the air around us.

762
00:56:09,460 --> 00:56:12,880
Radio is not so much invented as plucked from the air.

763
00:56:12,881 --> 00:56:17,680
It is hard to consider how far radio has come in just the 20th century

764
00:56:17,681 --> 00:56:22,260
without wondering what marvels await us in the next

765
00:56:22,261 --> 00:56:27,120
and realizing just how fast we are rushing to meet the future.

